WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.960
Okay guys, in this problem, a concrete gravity retaining wall is being used to support an

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:12.599
elevated section of very dense soil. The concrete has a unit weight of 140 pounds per cubic

00:00:12.599 --> 00:00:19.440
foot. Knowing that the factor of safety against overturning at 0.A is 3, what is the soil's

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:24.640
active force per unit length of the wall? If found according to the Rankine active

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:30.760
earth pressure theory. So the first thing we can do in this problem is go to pages 80

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:35.500
or 81 of our civil review manual and that's going to give us some equations that we're

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:45.840
going to want. The first one is going to be the equation for coefficient of active earth

00:00:45.840 --> 00:01:09.680
pressure and that's going to be KA equals tan squared 45 minus V over 2. The second equation

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:22.640
we're going to pull off of page 80 is going to be the active force within depth Z. So that's

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:36.980
going to be PA equals our term found above KA times gamma times the depth Z squared over

00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:55.060
2. PA is going to be active pressure and that is what we're going to be solving for in this

00:01:55.060 --> 00:02:04.620
problem. However, we can do that in this case. It notes that V is not given for this problem. So

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:08.980
we're not going to be able to calculate it using these two steps. If we had it, we could do

00:02:08.980 --> 00:02:18.099
that and we'd be done. In this case, we're going to solve for the moments of the wall about

00:02:18.099 --> 00:02:26.099
0.A. So holding back the soil, the wall is going to want to rotate this way and the soil itself is

00:02:26.099 --> 00:02:36.379
going to want to rotate this way pushing against the wall about 0.A. And knowing that the factor of safety

00:02:36.379 --> 00:02:51.819
is going to be the moment of the wall over the moment of the soil and it's going to equal 3. We can

00:02:51.900 --> 00:03:00.780
calculate the moment of the wall and the soil. Or rather, with the moment of the wall, then find the soil and

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:13.299
then from the moment in the soil, we can find the resultant force. So let's do it. The first step we're going to

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:25.300
go through here is going to be the rotational force or moment of the footing. Wall footing, same thing in

00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:32.620
this case. So let's break this into two areas. This is going to be area 1 for the triangle. This will be

00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:49.340
area 2 for the rectangle. So A1 here is going to be 1 1-1 foot and that's this base here we can see because

00:03:49.340 --> 00:03:59.379
there's one foot length here, two feet down here. Seven feet is the height of the triangle and then the unit weight

00:03:59.379 --> 00:04:08.859
is 140 pounds per cubic foot, stated in the problem. So that is the concrete weight per linear foot.

00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:29.060
Linear foot, just to be clear. And now if we want the distance about the rotational distance, we're going to need

00:04:29.060 --> 00:04:48.060
to do the total distance which is going to be again 1 about point, about point A times 2-thirds because

00:04:49.060 --> 00:05:03.060
centroid of the moment here will be 2-thirds away from this length of 1. So it's going to be 1 foot times 2-thirds.

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:17.060
And multiplying all of this together gets us 323 pound feet per linear foot of the wall.

00:05:17.060 --> 00:05:29.060
And now we can calculate area 2. So this is going to be again 1 foot with times 7 foot height,

00:05:32.060 --> 00:05:40.060
7 feet times 140 pounds per cubic foot.

00:05:41.060 --> 00:05:53.060
And again we need our distance which in this case it's going to be the length of 1 away from A and also an additional

00:05:53.060 --> 00:06:03.060
point 5 because the moment of rotation is about the center of a rectangle whereas it was 2-thirds away from the edge of this triangle.

00:06:03.060 --> 00:06:21.060
So let's say 1.5 feet and this will give us a much larger rotation of 1470 pounds feet per foot.

00:06:22.060 --> 00:06:38.060
We can combine these two since they are all contributing to get 1793 pound feet per linear foot of wall.

00:06:38.060 --> 00:06:46.060
Okay, so knowing that our factor of safety again of the concrete and the soil,

00:06:46.060 --> 00:07:04.060
just go back up here since we already wrote it out, equals this case that's going to be 1793 pounds feet per feet over

00:07:04.060 --> 00:07:11.060
a moment about A of the soil.

00:07:11.060 --> 00:07:22.060
So solving for that real fast gives us and I'll just write it down here.

00:07:22.060 --> 00:07:49.060
So we need to keep in mind that that's the rotational force and we were asked for the active earth pressure which is the PA.

00:07:49.060 --> 00:08:17.060
So PA in this case is going to be 597.8 pound foot per linear foot divided by and we need to use the height of the wall in this case

00:08:17.060 --> 00:08:29.060
as our distance but knowing that the force in the soil increases steadily as we go down.

00:08:29.060 --> 00:08:39.059
So in the same way that this triangle, it was 2-thirds of the length we're going to be using 2-thirds of the depth here.

00:08:39.059 --> 00:08:46.059
Also known as the bottom third is where that rotational point about A is going to be coming from.

00:08:46.059 --> 00:08:52.059
So it's going to be rotating at a depth of 1-third times 7.

00:08:52.059 --> 00:09:00.059
So 7 feet times 1-third and we did the math out for that.

00:09:00.059 --> 00:09:18.059
We will get 256.6 pound feet and that will be our active pressure for this problem.

00:09:18.059 --> 00:09:28.059
So there's also one more thing I wanted to add is how this problem could have been solved if P had been given instead.

00:09:28.059 --> 00:09:37.059
So let's say that P had been equal to 57 degrees instead of being not given as we could see here.

00:09:37.059 --> 00:09:41.059
So in this case we could start off the problem by calculating Ka.

00:09:41.059 --> 00:09:57.059
So that would have been tan squared of 45 minus 57 over 2 and that should have given us 0.087.

00:09:57.059 --> 00:10:03.059
And this is probably a lower than realistic coefficient of active pressure to be honest with you.

00:10:03.059 --> 00:10:08.059
I don't think I should have used a very dense soil when trying to design this problem.

00:10:08.059 --> 00:10:16.059
Most Ka is I believe are between 0.2 and 0.5 so this is a little low but the math will still work for the sake of this problem.

00:10:16.059 --> 00:10:19.059
So next we would calculate Pa.

